1	# num-overflow-explicit
2	
3	> Handle integer overflow explicitly: `checked_`/`saturating_`/`wrapping_`/`overflowing_`
4	
5	## Why It Matters
6	
7	Integer overflow panics in debug builds and silently wraps (two's complement) in release builds. Relying on either default behavior is a latent bug — the release build can produce wrong results without any diagnostic. Choosing an explicit variant makes intent unmistakable to both the compiler and future readers.
8	
9	## Bad
10	
11	```rust
12	fn add_score(current: u32, delta: u32) -> u32 {
13	    current + delta  // panics in debug, wraps silently in release
14	}
15	
16	fn increment_counter(c: u8) -> u8 {
17	    c + 1  // wraps to 0 in release when c == 255
18	}
19	```
20	
21	## Good
22	
23	```rust
24	// checked_add: returns None on overflow — propagate or handle the error
25	fn add_score(current: u32, delta: u32) -> Option<u32> {
26	    current.checked_add(delta)
27	}
28	
29	// saturating_add: clamps at the type's upper bound (u8::MAX == 255)
30	fn increment_saturating(c: u8) -> u8 {
31	    c.saturating_add(1)
32	}
33	
34	// wrapping_add: intentional modular (two's complement) arithmetic
35	fn wrapping_sequence(n: u8) -> u8 {
36	    n.wrapping_add(1)
37	}
38	
39	// overflowing_add: returns (result, did_overflow) — useful for carry detection
40	fn add_with_carry(a: u32, b: u32) -> (u32, bool) {
41	    a.overflowing_add(b)
42	}
43	
44	#[cfg(test)]
45	mod tests {
46	    use super::*;
47	
48	    #[test]
49	    fn checked_returns_none_on_overflow() {
50	        assert_eq!(add_score(u32::MAX, 1), None);
51	        assert_eq!(add_score(10, 5), Some(15));
52	    }
53	
54	    #[test]
55	    fn saturating_clamps_at_max() {
56	        assert_eq!(increment_saturating(255), 255);
57	        assert_eq!(increment_saturating(10), 11);
58	    }
59	
60	    #[test]
61	    fn wrapping_rolls_over() {
62	        assert_eq!(wrapping_sequence(255), 0);
63	    }
64	
65	    #[test]
66	    fn overflowing_reports_carry() {
67	        assert_eq!(add_with_carry(u32::MAX, 1), (0, true));
68	        assert_eq!(add_with_carry(1, 2), (3, false));
69	    }
70	}
71	```
72	
73	## Key Points
74	
75	| Method family | Returns | Use when |
76	|---|---|---|
77	| `checked_*` | `Option<T>` | overflow is an error your caller must handle |
78	| `saturating_*` | `T` | clamping at the type's bounds is correct behavior |
79	| `wrapping_*` | `T` | modular arithmetic is intentional (e.g., checksums, ring buffers) |
80	| `overflowing_*` | `(T, bool)` | you need the result *and* know whether it overflowed |
81	
82	These methods are available on all primitive integer types in the standard library (`u8` through `u128`, `i8` through `i128`, `usize`, `isize`). Equivalent sub/mul/div/shl/shr variants exist for every arithmetic operation.
83	
84	## See Also
85	
86	- [num-saturating-clamp](num-saturating-clamp.md) - bound values with `clamp` and saturating arithmetic
87	- [num-cast-try-from](num-cast-try-from.md) - avoid `as` for narrowing casts; prefer `TryFrom`
