1	# conv-fromstr-parsing
2	
3	> Implement `FromStr` to enable `str::parse` for string-to-type conversions
4	
5	## Why It Matters
6	
7	`FromStr` is the single standard hook for parsing a `&str` into a typed value. Implementing it unlocks the idiomatic `.parse::<T>()` call, integrates with CLI argument parsers (clap, argh), and is the expected interface for serde string-deserializable types. A bespoke `fn parse_foo(s: &str)` forces callers to learn a private name and breaks generic code that constrains `T: FromStr`.
8	
9	## Bad
10	
11	```rust
12	#[derive(Debug)]
13	enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }
14	
15	// Callers must know this private name; no `.parse()` support
16	fn parse_color(s: &str) -> Result<Color, String> {
17	    match s {
18	        "red"   => Ok(Color::Red),
19	        "green" => Ok(Color::Green),
20	        "blue"  => Ok(Color::Blue),
21	        other   => Err(format!("unknown color: {other}")),
22	    }
23	}
24	
25	fn main() {
26	    let c = parse_color("red").unwrap();
27	}
28	```
29	
30	## Good
31	
32	```rust
33	use std::str::FromStr;
34	use std::fmt;
35	
36	#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]
37	enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }
38	
39	#[derive(Debug)]
40	struct ParseColorError(String);
41	
42	impl fmt::Display for ParseColorError {
43	    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> fmt::Result {
44	        write!(f, "unknown color: {}", self.0)
45	    }
46	}
47	
48	impl std::error::Error for ParseColorError {}
49	
50	impl FromStr for Color {
51	    type Err = ParseColorError;
52	
53	    fn from_str(s: &str) -> Result<Self, Self::Err> {
54	        match s {
55	            "red"   => Ok(Color::Red),
56	            "green" => Ok(Color::Green),
57	            "blue"  => Ok(Color::Blue),
58	            other   => Err(ParseColorError(other.to_owned())),
59	        }
60	    }
61	}
62	
63	fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
64	    // Standard idiom — works with clap, config parsers, etc.
65	    let c: Color = "green".parse()?;
66	    assert_eq!(c, Color::Green);
67	    Ok(())
68	}
69	```
70	
71	## Notes
72	
73	- Use a concrete `Err` type, not `String`, so callers can pattern-match on parse failures.
74	- `FromStr` pairs naturally with `Display`: if you can parse it in, you should be able to print it out.
75	- For infallible string conversions (e.g., wrapping a `String` in a newtype), consider `From<&str>` or `From<String>` instead.
76	- CLI crates like `clap` detect `FromStr` automatically via the `value_parser` attribute macro.
77	
78	## See Also
79	
80	- [conv-tryfrom-fallible](conv-tryfrom-fallible.md) - `TryFrom` for fallible non-string conversions
81	- [type-newtype-validated](type-newtype-validated.md) - newtypes for validated data like `Email`, `Url`
82	- [api-parse-dont-validate](api-parse-dont-validate.md) - parse into validated types at boundaries
